name: recon
boss: sweep
flavor: Before you touch anything, you look.
brief: SAMPLE LEVEL -- proves the graded engine end to end. The real arsenal
brief: recon tools live on p3, not in this worktree, so these cards stand in
brief: with plain busybox commands (`find`, `grep`, `wc`, `touch`, `rm`) that
brief: exercise the same three graders: text match, run-and-judge in the jail,
brief: and state checks against the scratch tree afterward.
brief:
brief: Real-tool cards (the actual arsenal binaries) come later, once this
brief: engine is proven. Nothing here needs a network target.
---
q: list every file under @dir@, recursively
a: find @dir@
ref: find
teach: find
hint: one command, one argument -- the directory
---
q: count how many `.log` files are under @dir@
a: find @dir@ -name '*.log' | wc -l
out: ^3$
ref: find
teach: find
hint: find with -name, piped to wc -l
seed: mkdir -p @dir@
seed: touch @dir@/a.log @dir@/b.log @dir@/c.log @dir@/notes.txt @dir@/readme.txt
---
q: make an empty file named `found.txt` inside @dir@
a: touch @dir@/found.txt
ref: touch
teach: touch
hint: touch takes the path to create
state: exists:@dir@/found.txt
seed: mkdir -p @dir@
---
q: delete `junk.tmp` from @dir@
a: rm @dir@/junk.tmp
ref: rm
teach: rm
hint: rm and the path
state: gone:@dir@/junk.tmp
seed: mkdir -p @dir@
seed: touch @dir@/junk.tmp
---
q: search @dir@ recursively for the word @needle@
a: grep -r @needle@ @dir@
out: @needle@
ref: grep
teach: grep
hint: grep -r, the word, then the directory
seed: mkdir -p @dir@
seed: printf 'a line with @needle@ in it\n' > @dir@/hit.txt
seed: printf 'nothing to see here\n' > @dir@/miss.txt
---
q: print how many lines are in `notes.txt` inside @dir@
a: wc -l < @dir@/notes.txt
out: ^4$
ref: wc
teach: wc
hint: wc -l, redirected in
seed: mkdir -p @dir@
seed: printf 'one\ntwo\nthree\nfour\n' > @dir@/notes.txt
